# Sanitized OpenWrt WireGuard configuration example
#
# All cryptographic keys, production endpoint information, and DDNS
# credentials have been replaced with placeholders.
#
# The project used WireGuard for remote access to selected internal
# resources rather than exposing LuCI or SSH directly to the WAN.

# OpenWrt WireGuard interface

config interface 'wg0'
	option proto 'wireguard'
	option private_key '<SERVER-PRIVATE-KEY>'
	list addresses '10.0.40.1/24'
	option listen_port '51820'

# Example remote client peer

config wireguard_wg0
	option description 'Remote Client'
	option public_key '<CLIENT-PUBLIC-KEY>'
	option route_allowed_ips '1'
	list allowed_ips '10.0.40.2/32'

# Example client configuration
#
# This is shown for documentation only and is not intended to contain
# real production keys.

# [Interface]
# PrivateKey = <CLIENT-PRIVATE-KEY>
# Address = 10.0.40.2/32
# DNS = 10.0.10.1
#
# [Peer]
# PublicKey = <SERVER-PUBLIC-KEY>
# Endpoint = router.example.duckdns.org:51820
# AllowedIPs = 10.0.10.0/24, 10.0.40.0/24
# PersistentKeepalive = 25

# Firewall behavior used by the project:
#
# WAN UDP 51820
#     -> WireGuard server
#
# wg0
#     -> dedicated VPN firewall zone
#     -> permitted internal resources
#
# The VPN subnet is 10.0.40.0/24. It is a routed WireGuard network
# and is not an Ethernet VLAN.

# Remote-access validation was performed from an iPhone 15 using
# cellular data. The test confirmed a successful WireGuard handshake,
# remote OpenWrt management access, DNS resolution, and continued
# Internet connectivity while using the split-tunnel configuration.

# Never publish:
#
# - Server private key
# - Client private key
# - DDNS authentication token
# - Production backup containing these credentials
